{
  "id": "Compound::DB00741 - Disease::DOID:9074",
  "verbalization": "Hydrocortisone, a corticosteroid hormone receptor agonist, is implicated in the treatment of Systemic Lupus Erythematosus (SLE) through multiple pathways. The evidence suggests that Hydrocortisone and its metabolite Betamethasone, both belonging to the 20-Oxo Steroid class, are linked to SLE via their roles in modulating immune responses. Pathway 1 links Hydrocortisone to Prednisolone, which in turn is associated with SLE, while Pathway 2 and 3 connect Hydrocortisone to Betamethasone, which also contributes to SLE treatment. The shared lowest common ancestor, Hydrocortisone and Betamethasone, underscores a common biological context within the 20-Oxo Steroid class. These findings suggest that corticosteroids, through their receptor-mediated mechanisms, may influence immune regulation in SLE, though the exact mechanisms and therapeutic relevance require further investigation. The evidence highlights a potential role of corticosteroids in SLE pathophysiology, but the strength of this relationship is supported by multiple pathways and scores, indicating a plausible but not fully understood mechanism.",
